      "file": "molecular_sieve_adsorber_temperature.glb",
      "title": "Adsorption front and regeneration temperature",
      "description": "In the adsorbing tower water is held in the zeolite pores; the saturated zone grows from the inlet and the mass-transfer zone moves down until breakthrough, when the towers switch. Regeneration heats the bed (typically ~ 200–300 °C, TSA) to drive the water off, then cools it. Molecular sieves reach very low dew points (≈ −70 to −100 °C, < 1 ppmv water) — needed before cryogenic units.",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
